{"id": "1935", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=1935", "title": "Aufse\u00df", "text": "Aufse\u00df a municipality of 1,300 people (2019) in Upper Franconia. It is most well known as the village with the most beer breweries per capita, however it can be thought of as a set of hamlets along the St2188, each with their own brewery.\nThe community of Aufse\u00df has been listed in September 2000 in the Guinness Book of Records as world record-holder for\nthe greatest brewery density in the world.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest major airport to Aufse\u00df is in Nuremberg ().\nBuy.\nEvery brewery has its own beer stones and more things to buy.\nDrink.\nThere is a walk Brauereienweg around the 4 World Record Breweries of Aufse\u00df:\nsee Breweries in Franconia#Aufse\u00df\nSleep.\nFinding affordable accommodation might be difficult in summer.\nGo next.\nVisit Bamberg to see the medieval town and try the local smoked beer (Rauchbier) or to Bayreuth, is famous as the host of the Richard Wagner Festival, furthermore the Markgr\u00e4fliches Opernhaus is listed on the UNESCO World Heritage List or deeper into Franconian Switzerland Pottenstein."}
{"id": "1937", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=1937", "title": "Augrabies", "text": "Augrabies is a small town in the Northern Cape province of South Africa, situated on the south bank of the Orange River about 100 km (60 mi) downstream from Upington. It is located on the R359 road just outside the Augrabies Falls National Park, which contains the Augrabies Falls for which the town is named.\nGet in.\nFrom Cape Town, take the N7 north. Either take the R358 for a more direct route (about 10.5 hours in total, albeit along a dirt road much of the way), or continue north to Springbok, taking the N14 east to Augrabies (11 hours in total).\nAlternatively, fly to Upington airport, and drive the remaining 120km west.\nSee.\nWhen the Orange River has a lot of water, the Augrabies Falls turn into mighty falls that are really spectacular.\nDo.\nHot-air ballooning (in the cooler months only) and white-water rafting are available.\nThere are also a number of short hikes, and the 3-day Klipspringer hike (closed in summer) available inside the National Park.\nGo next.\nGo to Upington or Namibia or the Western Cape Province and Cape Town."}
{"id": "1938", "revid": "898440", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=1938", "title": "Augrabies Falls National Park", "text": "Augrabies Falls National Park is in the Northern Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nAugrabies Falls National Park is a national park located around its main attraction, the Augrabies Falls, about 120 km west of Upington. The park covers an area of 820 km\u00b2 and stretches along the Orange River. The area is very arid. The waterfall is about 60 m high and is awe-inspiring when the river is in flood. The gorge below the falls averages about 240 m deep and runs for 18 km. The gorge provides an impressive example of erosion into a granitic basement.\nHistory.\nEstablished in 1966, the original Khoikhoi people named the waterfall Ankoerebis, meaning the \"place of big noises\". The Trekboers who later settled in the area derived the name Augrabies. The name is sometimes spelt Aughrabies.\nLandscape.\nA prominent landmark in the park is Moon Rock, a large exfoliation dome measuring around 700 m (2,300 ft) by 100 m (330 ft) and 30 m (98 ft) high.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe most characteristic plant in the park is the giant aloe (Aloe dichotoma) known locally as the quiver tree or kokerboom. It is perfectly adapted to the dry semi-desert rocky areas found in the Nama-Karoo, able to withstand the extreme temperatures and the infertile soil. This tree, which grows up to five metres high, gets its name from the fact that the Bushmen (San) used the soft branches to make quivers for their arrows. The eye-catching silhouette of the quiver tree is typical of this part of Northern Cape landscape. When the tree flowers in the winter flocks of birds are attracted to their copious nectar, and baboons can be seen tearing the flowers apart to get the sweet liquor.\nFauna include giraffe, leopard, kudo, eland, steenbok, dassie, and an exceptionally high concentration of lizards. Vervet monkeys and baboons have become habituated to humans and will take any unattended food.\nClimate.\nExtremely hot in summer, with the December maximum averaging 36\u00b0C. Rooms are air-conditioned, but camping can be unpleasant, and many of the walks are not advisable in the heat.\nGet in.\nFrom Cape Town, take the N7 north. Either take the R358 for a more direct route (about 10\u00bd hours in total, albeit along a dirt road much of the way), or continue north to Springbok, taking the N14 east to Augrabies village (11 hours in total).\nAlternatively, fly to Upington airport, and drive the remaining 120 km.\nFees.\nVaild to 31 October 2025:\nGet around.\nOn foot.\nWalkways lead from the restaurant to the observation decks at the main falls.\nDo.\nTake an easy walk to the waterfalls from the park reception area. There are a number of lookout points. The falls are also lit up until 10pm for a spectacular (and much cooler) night view.\nHot-air ballooning (in the cooler months only) and white-water rafting are available outside of the park.\nThere are also short hikes, and the 3-day Klipspringer hike (closed in summer) available.\nBuy.\nAn ATM and petrol station is available at the main camp. The small shop next to the reception sells curios and some food supplies.\nEat.\nThere is a restaurant in the park serving mostly meat dishes, with limited vegetarian fare. The small shop also sells some food items.\nDrink.\nVisiting in the summer, you will need to drink a lot! The restaurant has a bar area, and the shop also sells alcohol Monday to Saturday.\nSleep.\nAll booking is done through the Sanparks website.\nAir-conditioned and comfortable \"chalets and cottages\" are available. Base rate for chalets range between R975 and R1715 per night for up to 2 people. Family cottages for up to 4 people range from R1765-2360 (Aug 2017).\nThe campsites are shaded and well-situated, but the area gets extremely hot in summer, so come prepared or rent an air-conditioned chalet. Base rate for camp site is R250 per night for up to 2 people (Aug 2017).\nStay safe.\nMost of the risks are of the wild variety, with baboons and vervet monkeys likely to steal unattended food (or raid a cottage left with its windows open). Care also needs to be taken to avoid scorpion stings and snakebites when walking in the park.\nThe park also falls into a marginal malaria area, but the risk is low and most visitors don't bother with prophylactics."}
{"id": "1939", "revid": "2161147", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=1939", "title": "Augsburg", "text": "Augsburg is a lovely metropolitan city of 300,000 people and around 885,000 in the urban area (2023) in the German \"Bundesland\" (Federal State) of Bavaria (German: \"Bayern\"). Some 60 km northwest of Munich, it is one of the oldest German cities. It boasts a rich history and has developed a modern urban vibe.\nUnderstand.\nAugsburg is the administrative capital and local center of Bavarian Swabia. From 1276 until nearly the end of the Holy Roman Empire, Augsburg was a \"free imperial city\", nominally subject to nobody but the Emperor but \"de facto\" self-governing as a city state. Despite being hidden in Munich's overwhelming shadow, the city has a lot to offer. While many Germans might think the \"aug\" part may refer to eyes (Auge (n) in German) it actually goes back to its Roman name \"Augusta Vindelicorum\".\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nAugsburg is most easily reached by train from Munich Airport (). Take the S-Bahn \u00a0S1\u00a0 or \u00a0S8\u00a0 to \"M\u00fcnchen Hauptbahnhof\" (about 45 min), then a regional train to Augsburg. Plan 2\u20132\u00bd hours in total. A bus or private transfer can be faster if you are a group or have a lot of luggage.\nBuy a Bayern-Ticket at the airport station machines or in the DB Navigator app. It covers the S-Bahn and the regional train to Augsburg, plus trams and buses in the city. It is not valid on ICE/IC. 2026 price: \u20ac34 for the first person, \u20ac10 for each extra person (max. five) in 2nd class. Valid weekdays from 09:00, weekends and public holidays from 00:00, until 03:00 the next day. Bayern-Ticket Nacht: \u20ac32 / +\u20ac7, from 18:00 (until 06:00; Friday\u2013Saturday until 07:00). A Deutschland-Ticket is valid the same way every day.\nOther major airports within reach are Stuttgart ('), Nuremberg (') and Frankfurt, all with train links (expect at least one change) and an easy drive. For low-cost airlines, Memmingen Airport () is used by Ryanair and Wizz Air. FMM is under an hour by car, about 1\u00bd\u20132 hours by public transport.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance coaches do not use the Hauptbahnhof. The stop is the park-and-ride on the north side of town.\nBy car.\nAbout 60\u201380 km from Munich and 160 km from Stuttgart, just south of the A8 (Munich\u2013Stuttgart). From the north (Donauw\u00f6rth, Romantic Road) use the B2; from the south (Landsberg, Allg\u00e4u) the B17. Both federal roads run into town. The old town is a parking problem \u2014 use a P+R (West, Nord, Hochzoll) and ride the tram in. Parking in town is expensive.\nGet around.\nLocal transport is the AVV (Augsburger Verkehrs- und Tarifverbund): trams, city buses and regional trains inside the Augsburg fare area. It does not cover through tickets to Munich or Ulm - for those you need DB / a Bayern-Ticket / a Deutschland-Ticket. The same two state tickets are valid on AVV trams and buses, so you do not buy a second city ticket if you already hold one.\nInnenraum (zones 10\u201320, the city) is what most visitors need. From 1 January 2026: single \u20ac4.20, short hop (\"Kurzstrecke\", stop plus four) cheaper, day ticket \u20ac10.20 (extra adults +\u20ac3.60). Buy in the meinAVV or swa Mobil app, at machines, or on the bus. Paper tickets must be stamped; the app is already valid. No ticket (\"Schwarzfahren\") is a \u20ac60 fine.\nBy tram.\nFive tram lines: 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6. They meet at K\u00f6nigsplatz and Hauptbahnhof. Roughly 04:30\u201300:30. Line 3 is the one for the university (Universit\u00e4t); 4 for Oberhausen Nord / the FlixBus P+R; 6 for the Textilmuseum / Friedberg direction. Special lines 8 (WWK Arena) and 9 (Messe) run only on match and fair days.\nBy bus.\nCity buses fill the gaps the rails miss (zoo and botanical garden: bus 32). Thursday\u2013Sunday night buses cover the hours after the last tram, so you get a thin 24-hour service those nights, not a full grid. Village buses outside the city can be hourly or worse on Sunday.\nBy train.\nUse this for suburbs, not for the old town. Regional trains on the trunk Hochzoll \u2013 Haunstetterstra\u00dfe \u2013 Hbf \u2013 Oberhausen are frequent. The BRB and other RB/RE lines reach Friedberg, Mering, Bobingen, Neus\u00e4\u00df, Inningen and the Messe halt. Same AVV ticket inside the verbund; to Munich or Ulm buy DB / Bayern-Ticket / Deutschland-Ticket. \nBy car.\nUseful for the A8, B2 and B17 and for villages. In the centre it is a parking hunt. Use P+R West, Nord or Hochzoll and the tram. The Altstadt is mostly residents-only or short-stay.\nBy bike.\nWith its mostly flat terrain, Augsburg is great to explore by bike. That way, you can more easily visit some hidden gems in the suburbs and along the rivers Lech and Wertach. Augsburg's UNESCO World Heritage (\"Water Management System of Augsburg\") also includes several objects outside the centre.\nBikes can be rented on several bus stations from the \"Stadtwerke\", and at some bike stores in Augsburg:\nLearn.\nAugsburg offers a dynamic academic environment with opportunities for intellectual growth through its higher education institutions. The city\u2019s universities and colleges provide public lectures, workshops, and open events covering diverse fields like humanities, sciences, engineering, music, and business. Visitors can engage in trial study days, interdisciplinary seminars, or specialized masterclasses, particularly in areas like sustainability, digitalization, and music performance. Many institutions also host cultural and academic events, fostering a vibrant learning atmosphere.\nBuy.\nAugsburg is a lively university town, a city for business and recreation. Enjoy the benefits like traditional pubs, theater, museums and lots of shopping facilities. You find many retail shops in the pedestrian zone, but don't miss to go downhill (e.g. via Judenberg) to the \"Altstadt\" with its unique shops! There is also a larger mall (City Galerie) with common brands and big stores, busy on weekends. For souvenirs, go to the Rathausplatz, find Augsburg-umbrellas and Brecht T-shirts. The local brewery Riegele sells giftboxes with 6 types of beer!\nEat.\nAugsburg is famous for its plum cake, called Zwetschgendatschi, and Bosna, a sausage coming in a bun with hot sauce and onions. Also, try K\u00e4sspatzen (which literally translates as \"cheese sparrows\", but no birds are harmed during preparation), a popular hand made pasta dish topped with melted mountain cheese and served with caramelized onions.\nDrink.\nIn Augsburg there are several beer gardens, and some of them have their own children's playground. What is written in the Munich travel guide about beer gardens also applies to Augsburg's beer gardens. The Riegele beer garden, close to the central train station and right next to the Riegele brewery, is a good place to go.\nClubs and bars.\nA good part of Augsburg's night life takes place in Maximilianstra\u00dfe and the historic district right beneath the Maximilianstra\u00dfe. There is great diversity in the type of clubs and bars, ranging from disco to student bars. It is a great idea to go see more than one place and find out which is the most comforting to you. Luckily, in generally, Augsburg's night life is a lot cheaper than in Munich or other big cities.\nSleep.\nBudget.\nAugsburg has some cheap places to stay. One is the Jugendherberge the other is a private hostel\nStay safe.\nAugsburg is very safe; in 2017, it was ranked as the second safest German city with over 200,000 inhabitants. Virtually all crimes are domestic violence (which are quite rare), drug abuse, or alcohol-fueled fights at a bar. Augsburg also tends to be pretty safe at night. Don't be afraid to use the small and poorly lit alleys in the historic district, since these are beautiful especially after dark!"}
